{"type": "thinking", "text": "Restored favorite. Now, let me test the \"Media tab content-type chips\" more. The nav label change is obvious. Let me also see whether the chip choice affects the \"Default Startup Tab\" or the \"Videos\" label. I'll report the nav label bug.\n\nBut wait — before reporting, is the nav label really wrong? Let's think about the app design: Maybe the first tab is intended to show the current media type as its label, e.g., not a \"Media\" tab but a content-type tab that says \"Media\"/\"Photos\"/\"Videos\". The Settings \"Navigation tabs\" section shows \"Media\" as the tab name, and \"Default Startup Tab\" shows \"Media\". So the canonical name is Media. The nav label changing to Photos/Videos is inconsistent. Additionally, the nav icon changes (play icon for Videos). Hmm, maybe the design *is* that the tab shows what content it contains? That would be unusual. I'm fairly convinced it's a bug: the chip state leaks into the bottom navigation label and icon.\n\nActually, wait. Let me re-examine the first screenshot: bottom nav first icon was a stack of photos labeled \"Media\". When I selected \"Photos\" chip, the nav showed \"Photos\" with what icon? Let me check the screenshot with Favorites when Videos chip: the first nav icon was a play button in a rounded square. That's a different icon. So the nav item is dynamically rendering the content type. This could be intentional: when you switch to Videos, the \"Media\" tab's identity changes to Videos. But then \"Media\" is a category of all types; \"Photos\" and \"Videos\" are filters within. The tab label changing would imply the tab becomes a \"Videos\" section.\n\nHmm, let me look at the screen title: with Photos chip selected, the top title is \"Photos\". The bottom tab also \"Photos\". Maybe the app intentionally renames both title and tab. But the Settings says the tab is \"Media\". A user would be confused. As QA, I'd report it as inconsistent navigation labeling. The \"changed\" note \"the chosen chip is now tracked by the screen rather than in the app's settings\" suggests the chip state moved into the screen and is now (wrongly) used to label the tab. I think this is a seeded bug. Let me file it.\n\nBut before that, let me look for other bugs so I can batch reports. Also, I should check the \"favorites grid\" changed behavior for a seeded bug. Let me think about how \"favorites queried through the same data source as Media tab\" could introduce a bug:\n- The Media tab data source might be filtered by the current content type (Photos/Videos). If Favorites uses the same source *instance* with the filter, favorites would be filtered by the Media chip. We tested: with Videos chip, Favorites showed photos. Hmm, but wait — did it? Earlier when Videos chip was selected, I opened Favorites and saw big.jpg (the favorite) while the nav label said Videos. Yes it showed the photo. So not filtered.\n- The Media tab data source might exclude items in certain folders (e.g., excluded folders) or include trash. Favorites would then miss favorites in excluded folders. None.\n- The Media tab may sort by date added; favorites sort might be expected by favorite date. Not a bug.\n- The Media data source may not include \"locked\" vault items. If you favorite a locked item, it disappears from favorites. Can't test.\n- The Media data source might use MediaStore *images* only, while favorites previously included videos. No videos.\n- The favorites grid might show *duplicates* when an item is in multiple data sources. No.\n\nHmm. Maybe the bug is in the favorites grid's *empty state* or count. Let me favorite all 6 items and see if all 6 appear. That tests completeness. Then unfavorite them (restore). That's a bit of work but doable via the Media viewer: start at item 1, tap Favorite, next, favorite... Actually the Favorite button is in the action bar; after favoriting, navigate via Next (details sheet) or the viewer's swipe. The viewer header has \"Next\" only in Details sheet. Hmm, the viewer top has no next arrow except maybe swipe. Swipe didn't work earlier (maybe because I used x,y on the image but it's a pager with horizontal drag; the tool's swipe might not register). Alternatively, tap the filmstrip thumbnails to navigate. For 6 items, doable.\n\nBefore that, consider: do I need to favorite all? The favorites data source bug might show only a subset (e.g., only first album). Let's try favoriting the remaining 4 items and see if favorites count = 6. If some missing, that's a bug. Original favorites: big.jpg (Beta) and hughugeQ.jpg (Beta). Now we moved big.jpg to Beta/big (1).jpg (favorited). So favorites: Beta/big (1).jpg, Beta/hughugeQ.jpg. Let's favorite the other 4: IrisSeed/huge.jpg, IrisSeed/big.jpg, Zulu/hughugeQ.jpg, apple/big.jpg.\n\nWe're in viewer item 3 (big (1).jpg). I can use the filmstrip to jump: item 1 huge.jpg (element 7), item 2 big.jpg (element 8), item 4 hughugeQ (element 10), item 5 big.jpg apple (element 11), item 6 hughugeQ Beta (element 12).\n\nFavorite item 1: tap element 7."}
