{"type": "thinking", "text": "Favorites restored to 2. Good.\n\nNow, favorites area seems functional. The only oddity is nav label \"Photos\".\n\nLet me reconsider the media tab chip leak. It's clearly reproducible. Let me also test whether changing the chip while on another tab (e.g., going to Media, changing chip) then pressing back to a previous tab uses the chip. The nav label changes globally (seen on Favorites/Albums). That's the bug.\n\nCould there be an intended \"state\" bug where navigating to another tab and back loses the chip? Earlier it persisted. Let me test after switching to Media from another tab repeatedly: currently chip Photos. Tap Albums (goes to Beta detail), then Media (Photos still). Yes persisted.\n\nLet me test after rotating on Favorites: the nav label \"Photos\" persists; fine.\n\nNow, let me think about \"Album grid header: the item count is now computed once when the album finishes loading.\" Maybe the bug is that the count is computed from the *photo count* when the album first loads, but if the album has a mix of photos+videos, counts wrong. No videos though. Could we create a video? No.\n\nMaybe the bug is about the album grid *header* in the Albums screen: The \"header\" could be the top row of chips (\"All\", \"Camera & System\", \"Apps\", \"Videos\")? No, the item count?\n\nHmm, let me look at the Album detail screen more: Is there any header that should show item count but doesn't? The album detail header is just \"Beta\". In Material 3 gallery apps, the album screen header shows title and maybe item count. The change note says \"Album grid header: the item count is now computed once when the album finishes loading.\" \"Album grid header\" might refer to the header *above the album grid* on the Albums screen, i.e., no.\n\nWait, maybe when you open an album, the grid has a date header \"Today\" (element 0). The \"item count\" could be in that header? No.\n\nAlternatively \"Album grid header\" = the album card header (title/count). The count computed once when album finishes loading. Our counts are correct at this moment. Let me test a stale count scenario without moving: The album grid is currently in memory. If I open an album and favorite something (no count change), return, counts same. To test staleness, I'd need to change item membership. Moving did change counts and they updated — because the app rebuilt the album list. Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe the bug appears when you *delete* a photo (not move). The move may be handled differently (rebuild). Deletion might update the album detail but not the album card count, if the grid caches counts. Let me test deletion using trash and then restore. That's the likely intended test for \"item count computed once\". Let's do it:\n\n1. Note current album counts: IrisSeed 2, Beta cards: one with big (1).jpg (1), one with hughugeQ (1), apple 1, Zulu 1. (Not original, but fine.)\n2. Open a Beta card (big (1).jpg), delete the photo (Trash). \n3. Back to album grid, check whether the Beta card still shows \"1 items\" (stale) or disappears/0.\n4. Restore from Trash via Library → Trash → Restore.\n\nBut deletion of a favorite might complicate; big (1).jpg is favorited. Deleting moves it to trash; favorites then should drop it. Restoring should bring back favorite state? Maybe not, but that's a data change. Hmm.\n\nMaybe use an un-favorited photo: apple/big.jpg or Zulu/hughugeQ.jpg. Choose apple/big.jpg. Delete it, check apple count, then restore.\n\nActually, wait. Before destructive testing, let me check the item count display in the album grid while an album is loading. Not feasible.\n\nLet me examine \"Sort\" in Albums — maybe sorting by item count? no.\n\nAnother possibility: the item count bug is that the album grid count doesn't include the album's items when the item is a video, but no videos.\n\nHmm, let me look at the album grid counts vs. actual album content for all 5 albums. We know IrisSeed 2 (detail likely 2: huge.jpg, big.jpg). Beta card big (1).jpg 1 (detail 1). Beta hughugeQ 1 (detail 1). apple 1 (detail 1). Zulu 1 (detail 1). All correct. So item counts are fine currently.\n\nGiven the preconditions (IrisSeed with 2 photos), maybe the seeded bug relates to IrisSeed's count: perhaps it initially showed wrong count or missing until rescan. But now it's 2 items, correct.\n\nHmm, wait. The precondition says \"The gallery has an album IrisSeed with two photos: big.jpg (exactly 1,500,000 bytes) and huge.jpg (exactly 2,500,000 bytes).\" At session start, the album list did NOT include IrisSeed. That's a bug in album browsing! The precondition guarantees the album exists. The initial Albums tab showed only Beta, Zulu, apple. The IrisSeed album appeared only after a rescan. The Media tab also didn't show IrisSeed photos initially. This is a \"missing album/media\" bug. But is it reproducible? The state was at startup; now it's fixed. I could maybe restart the app? Video: force-stop via settings? I can use Android's app info → force stop. That would restart the app fresh and maybe reproduce the stale state? No — the DB now has IrisSeed items, so they'll show.\n\nGiven I can't reproduce, skip.\n\nHmm, hold on. Maybe the initial observation's media list of 4 was not stale; maybe the app's media tab was *filtered* by the \"Photos\" chip vs \"Media\" chip, and \"Photos\" excluded... no, both had 4.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the 2 IrisSeed files were *created by my move* at 11:42? Let me re-check: the Added time for huge.jpg is 11:42, and the current clock is ~11:53? The session started at 11:42. My move happened around 11:47-11:50. A newly created copy would have Added time = now (11:47+), not 11:42. Unless MediaStore copies original timestamps (DATE_ADDED preserved). The moved big (1).jpg had Added 11:19 (original's added time), so MediaStore preserved the timestamps when the app did its copy! Therefore, created files could preserve Added=11:42 if the source file was added at 11:42. But there was no source file big.jpg/huge.jpg in IrisSeed before. Hmm, unless the app *moved files out of a hidden folder*? No.\n\nWait, could the move operation have moved the entire beta album into IrisSeed? No, we moved one file to apple.\n\nLet me check the \"Modified\" timestamps: huge.jpg Modified 11:42, Added 11:42. Original fixture probably created at 11:42. My session start was 11:42 too. So the files were seeded just as the session began, likely before the app was launched. The app's first media scan may have been racing. So initial stale list is likely an artifact of the harness seeding data simultaneously with app launch. Not a reproducible app bug. Fine.\n\nOK so maybe the seeded bugs are subtle. Let me re-read the three changes and think like a developer introducing regressions:\n\n1. \"Album grid header: the item count is now computed once when the album finishes loading.\"\nBefore: count probably derived from the album's items list (reactive). After: computed once at load. Bug: if the album's content changes (photo added/removed) while the album grid remains in memory, the header count stays stale. To test: change album content without rebuilding the album list. Moving did rebuild. Deleting might not. Let's test deletion.\n\n2. \"Favorites grid: favorites are now queried through the same data source as the Media tab.\"\nBefore: favorites from a separate favorites store/query. After: same source. Bug possibility: the media data source might be filtered by hidden/excluded folders or content type, or might not include all favorites. Or the favorites query might now return ALL media (if the filter isn't applied correctly). We saw it correctly returns favorites only. Hmm. Maybe the bug appears for favorites added *before* the change (persisted favorites from previous build)? Our favorites were added now. Hmm. Maybe favorites added in the *album* viewer vs. *media* viewer? We added via both (Beta viewer, Favorites viewer) — works.\n\nWait, maybe the bug: opening Favorites passes the *favorite* list, but the viewer's header count \"1 of 6\" uses the Media data source (all 6) rather than the favorites list. We tested with 6 favorites so indistinguishable. Earlier with 2 favorites, opening from Favorites gave \"1 of 2\" — correct. So not.\n\nMaybe: favoriting an item from the Media tab while the \"Videos\" chip is ", "truncated": true}
