{"type": "thinking", "text": "Confirmed and logged. Great. Now continue testing.\n\nLet me think about other bugs. The developer confirmed this one. There may be more.\n\nThe album duplicate cards after delete/restore is a real bug. It was reproduced. Should I report it? It's in album browsing. Let me reproduce it again cleanly (now data is merged) and file it. Steps:\n- Albums → IrisSeed (2 items) → open a photo (big.jpg or huge.jpg) → Delete (trash) → back to album grid → IrisSeed count 1 (correct) → Library → Trash → restore → Albums → IrisSeed appears twice (\"huge.jpg | IrisSeed | 1 items\" and \"big.jpg | IrisSeed | 1 items\"). Then rescan to merge (restore state).\n\nBut this requires several steps. Is the bug worth reporting? The task's \"Changed in this build\" for album grid header count maybe hints that after restore the count should be recomputed, but instead a duplicate album appears. Hmm.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider the \"duplicate album\" phenomenon. Maybe it's not the restore/move operation per se but the \"Album grid header item count computed once\" bug: after restore, the album grid header (count) isn't recomputed, so the album's items are displayed in two cards? No, that doesn't make sense; the grid renders one card per album record.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the app stores albums in its own DB keyed by *bucket ID*, and the restore creates a new bucket ID because the app's trash restore writes the file back to the same path but MediaStore assigns a new bucket ID. The UI then shows two albums with the same display name. The \"correct\" behavior would be to merge by path. This is a legitimate bug.\n\nHowever, is it in the \"changed areas\"? \"Album browsing\" is a sweep area. Yes. I'll report it as duplicated album cards.\n\nBut before that, let me check whether the duplicate album is reproducible without delete/restore using only the *changed* feature. Could the \"count computed once\" cause duplicate album cards when the album list updates