{"type": "thinking", "text": "Aha! After restoring big (1).jpg, the Favorites grid still shows ONLY hughugeQ.jpg. The restored photo is no longer favorited! The favorite was lost on trash-restore. Is this a bug? Hmm. The photo was favorited, deleted to trash, then restored. The favorite flag was not preserved. In many apps, restoring from trash should restore the photo's metadata including favorite status. But arguably the app's trash restore is a re-import, losing metadata. Is this the seeded favorites bug? The changed note: \"Favorites grid: favorites are now queried through the same data source as the Media tab.\" If the favorites are stored in a separate favorites table keyed by media ID, deleting and restoring creates a new ID, so the favorite is lost. This could be the regression: previously favorites were stored by *path* (or the favorites grid queried the favorite table directly), so restoring the same path preserved the favorite. Now, querying through the Media data source with new IDs loses it. Hmm, plausible.\n\nBut is this a \"favorites\" bug we should report? The expected behavior: after restoring a deleted favorite, it should reappear in Favorites. The observed: it doesn't; the restored photo is not favorited. Let me verify the photo is in Media and not favorited: open it from Media/Albums and check the button says \"Favorite\" not \"Favorited\". Also, maybe the Favorites grid just didn't refresh; let me switch tabs and return. It might update. Let me check by opening the photo from Albums (Beta) — is it \"Favorite\"?\n\nFirst, switch to Albums and open big (1).jpg. But the Beta album now includes big (1).jpg again? Wait, after restore, the album split likely happened again (new bucket). And Beta album (2 items) may have been split. Let me check Albums.\n\nTap Albums (element 7)."}
